{"name":"Roman Mosaic","hex":"#dad1ce","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"roman","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Upright, as opposed to italic.","Of or related to the Latin alphabet or roman numerals."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the main three types used for the Latin alphabet (the others being italics and blackletter), in which the ascenders are mostly straight.","Ellipsis of roman numeral."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/roman"},{"term":"mosaic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of artwork created by placing colored pieces (usually tiles, either regular or irregular in shape) in a pattern so as to create a picture.","Pixelization."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Containing cells of varying genetic constitution."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mosaic"}],"entries":[{"title":"Roman mosaic","blocks":[{"text":"A Roman mosaic is a mosaic made during the Roman period, throughout the Roman Republic and later Empire. Mosaics were used in a variety of private and public buildings, on both floors and walls, though they competed with cheaper frescos for the latter. They were highly influenced by earlier and contemporary Hellenistic Greek mosaics, and often included famous figures from history and mythology, such as Alexander the Great in the Alexander Mosaic."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Mosaic_of_Neptune_and_Amphitrite.jpg/330px-Mosaic_of_Neptune_and_Amphitrite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_mosaic"},{"title":"Mosaic","blocks":[{"text":"A mosaic is a pattern or image made of small regular or irregular pieces of colored stone, glass or ceramic, held in place by plaster/mortar, and covering a surface. Mosaics are often used as floor and wall decoration, and were particularly popular in the Ancient Roman world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/MosaicEpiphany-of-Dionysus.jpg/330px-MosaicEpiphany-of-Dionysus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mosaic"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Pompeii_-_Cave_Canem_%284786638740%29.jpg/500px-Pompeii_-_Cave_Canem_%284786638740%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Pompeii_-_Cave_Canem_%284786638740%29.jpg/1280px-Pompeii_-_Cave_Canem_%284786638740%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Pompeii_-_Cave_Canem_%284786638740%29.jpg/500px-Pompeii_-_Cave_Canem_%284786638740%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Roman mosaic inscribed with the Latin phrase cave canem (\"beware of the dog\"), from the House of the Tragic Poet in Pompeii, Italy, 2nd century BC","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APompeii_-_Cave_Canem_(4786638740).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/11852_-_Vatican_-_Pius-Clementine_Museum_%283482901750%29.jpg/500px-11852_-_Vatican_-_Pius-Clementine_Museum_%283482901750%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/11852_-_Vatican_-_Pius-Clementine_Museum_%283482901750%29.jpg/500px-11852_-_Vatican_-_Pius-Clementine_Museum_%283482901750%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mosaic with Xenia, 4th century AD, Pius Clementine museum, Vatican Museums","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A11852_-_Vatican_-_Pius-Clementine_Museum_(3482901750).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Alexandermosaic.jpg/500px-Alexandermosaic.jpg?ut